WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.650
Let's get into audience questions for the
week, starting with, hey, David, what do you

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:10.520
believe is the future of the progressive movement
in the Democratic Party or is the future of

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.570
American progressivism outside of the Democratic
Party?

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.130
So there's a philosophical debate here.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:24.820
There's an ideological debate to be had about
will the Democratic Party allow itself to

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:31.940
become a truly progressive party or will it
not allow that meaning that if progressives

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:36.350
want a real foothold in the United States,
they will have to work outside the Democratic

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:41.780
Party as third party candidates or whatever
else the case may be?

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:43.030
Beautiful conversation.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.420
I love it.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.019
Completely irrelevant.

00:00:46.019 --> 00:00:54.369
If if campaign finance is not reformed and
arguably if we don't actually change our system

00:00:54.369 --> 00:00:59.440
of elections away from a first past, the post
winner take all system.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:06.630
I think that if you look at the Tea Party
movement of 2010, if the Tea Party movement

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:12.410
had decided to work exclusively outside of
the Republican Party instead of just having

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:18.570
Tea Partiers run as Republicans, had the Tea
Party succeeded at taking seats, it actually

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:21.070
would have weakened the Republican Party.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:26.500
And by its very but by by the inverse, it
would have strengthened the Democratic Party.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.770
Why is that?

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:32.530
If the Tea Party candidates were not running
as Republicans, they would have been running

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:33.649
against Republicans.

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:36.460
They would have disproportionately taken votes
from Republicans.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:40.950
They would have played spoilers for Republicans,
and they likely wouldn't have won as many

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:41.950
seats either.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:42.950
Right.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:46.590
So you would have had some cases where Republican
seats would have been lost to Tea Party seats

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.100
as third parties, and that would have weakened
the Republican Party.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:55.249
You also would have had seats that a Tea Partier
running inside the Republican Party could

00:01:55.249 --> 00:02:00.289
have won, but would not have because Republican
money would have poured into the Republican

00:02:00.289 --> 00:02:01.990
candidate and not the Tea Party candidate.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:05.630
So because campaign finance is what it is
and because we have a voting system, that

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:07.219
is what we have.

00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:14.260
The Tea Party won a bunch of seats in 2010
in the midterms because they ran as Republicans

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.250
at the local level, some didn't, but nationally
they ran as Republicans.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:25.159
Eventually, the Tea Party became integrated
into the Republican Party and the Republican

00:02:25.159 --> 00:02:28.650
Party overall was moved towards the Tea Party.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.090
This was a massive win for the Tea Party.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.489
You don't really hear much about the Tea Party
anymore.

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:36.269
It's just part of the Republican Party at
this point in time.

00:02:36.269 --> 00:02:39.939
That's an example of a success at changing
the party.

00:02:39.939 --> 00:02:46.519
Now, there's an argument to be made that Democrats
are not Republicans, that the Democratic Party

00:02:46.519 --> 00:02:54.269
would not ever be so influenced by a progressive
wing like the Tea Party was able to influence

00:02:54.269 --> 00:02:57.000
the Republican Party because they're just
fundamentally different parties.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.140
I completely disagree with this.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:06.540
And the evidence is look at how the Democratic
Party has already moved in the last five years

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:12.040
since Bernie Sanders in 2015 started more
prominently talking about Medicare for all

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.810
free college to a lesser extent, then more
in 2020, minimum wage increase.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:26.370
A lot of the stuff that was made prominent
by progressives back in 2015, 2016, 2017 is

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.770
now relatively mainstream in the Democratic
Party.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:32.969
Now, there is no question that because the
Democratic Party, as much as the Republican

00:03:32.969 --> 00:03:39.440
Party, are very corporate influenced establishment
parties, you still see, you know, a majority

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.959
of Democratic senators don't support something
like Medicare for all right now.

00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:50.439
But I think the evidence is clear that the
progressive wing progressives have already

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:51.940
influenced the Democratic Party.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:58.060
So my position is, if we don't change campaign
finance, move to public funding of elections,

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:01.689
limit the duration of a campaign, limit how
much money can be spent.

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:04.019
Eliminate PACs, all that different stuff.

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:08.810
I don't believe progressives have a serious
chance outside of the Democratic Party.

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:10.170
It pains me to say it.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:16.840
There's actually also a backfire risk if progressives
outside of the Democratic Party did a good

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:23.700
job of taking some seats away from Democrats
running independent third party in the short

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:30.030
and even medium term, it probably helps Republicans
by strengthening them in comparison to the

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:34.350
Democratic Party, the progressive wing, these
progressive independents would be too small

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.120
to really get very much done in the long term.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.200
It's a question mark and we don't know.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:46.390
So right now, if progressives were to break
off and form their own party, it could very

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:48.210
much damage the Democratic Party.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.130
If you're an acceleration ist right.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:55.620
If you prefer Trump over Biden, if it can't
be Bernie, then maybe you like that.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.620
Maybe you want to see the Democratic Party
destroyed.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:03.740
In order to at some point in the future build
it back up, that's a philosophical discussion.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:09.510
In practice, I don't see any way that if we
if we don't reform public campaign finance

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.150
and if we don't reform how our election systems
work.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:17.710
I don't see any way that progressive succeed
at the national level.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:21.610
Local is different by working outside the
Democratic Party.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:26.150
Go, go bigger than the Democratic Party, of
course.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:29.600
But in terms of electoral politics, you've
got to fix campaign finance and how we do

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:30.600
elections first.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:36.330
If you want progressives to have a shot outside
the Democratic Party at really making an influence.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:37.330
Hey, David.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:41.170
Does the hashtag Never Biden movement help
or hurt progressives?

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:47.240
So there are elements to this question that
are similar to the elements of whether the

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:50.730
progressive wing of the Democratic Party should
simply separate from the Democratic Party

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:51.730
altogether.

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:52.730
First and foremost, the never Biden people
who insist Biden can't win.

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:53.730
These are the exact same folks who believed
it, even when it was very clear Bernie had

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:54.730
no shot to the nomination that Bernie was
going to be the nominee.

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:55.730
So in terms of their ability to do political
prognostication, I would not put too much

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:56.810
weight behind the opinions of the hashtag.

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:57.810
Never Biden movement.

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:02.590
But I think that they bring an important question
to the table, which is over the long term.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:03.700
Over the long term.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:05.070
Does the progressive movement benefit or not
from Joe Biden winning in 2020?

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.620
Now, I did a segment about this a couple of
months ago.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.290
I am no stranger to this idea.

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:10.450
If you sort of take a game theory perspective,
you can argue that the best thing for progressives

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:12.270
is for Biden to be the nominee, but to lose
to Trump.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:13.620
Why could that be the best thing for progressives
over the long term?

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:14.620
This is theoretical.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:15.620
Well, Biden's the nominee and loses.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:16.620
It gives progressives the strongest position
to say, we told you, we told you an establishment

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:17.620
Democrat can't win.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:18.620
We need an actual progressive as the nominee.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:20.470
And it would potentially position a real progressive
for 2024 to potentially become the next president

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:21.470
of the United States.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:22.470
After Donald Trump's second term.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:23.470
Right.

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:24.470
That's the game theory question now.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:25.470
In practical terms, how much damage is done
for decades if Donald Trump gets to pick one

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:26.470
or two more Supreme Court justices during
his second term?

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:27.470
But that's not trivial.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:28.470
How much damage is done to the environment
if we get four years of Trump versus four

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:29.470
years of Biden, who supports the Green New
Deal as a framework?

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:30.470
If not fully, admittedly, how much does our
economy suffer if we get four more years of

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:31.470
Donald Trump's tax plan as opposed to Joe
Biden's, which is better on marginal tax rates,

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:32.470
which is better on the taxing of capital gains?

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:33.470
I could go on.

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:34.680
So I believe that if you want to ignore the
damage and destruction that Donald Trump will

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.890
do in a second term, in a game theory, hypothetical
perspective, there is a very good case to

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:41.250
be made that in the long run, maybe the progressive
movement is more jumpstarted by Biden losing

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:42.250
in November than winning.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:44.810
In practical terms, as a person who gets one
vote and has the decision to make, do I take

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:46.960
an action that reinstall as Trump or do I
take an action that removes him?

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:47.960
I have to vote for Joe Biden.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:48.960
That's my moral compass.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:49.960
I can't take an action that would make it
more likely Trump gets four more years and

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:50.960
this gets us to incrementalism versus acceleration
ism.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.730
When I look at history and I've done this
before, I won't I won't bore people by doing

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:56.730
it again.

00:06:56.730 --> 00:07:01.030
When people say, hey, let's bloods, burn it
down to build it back up.

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:04.940
I don't see instances in the last hundred
and fifty years of American history where

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:06.300
that was actually what worked.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.650
Other than through cataclysm and massive death,
when we look at the Progressive Era, the New

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:10.990
Deal era and the civil rights era.

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:12.090
Yes, there were protests.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:13.810
Yes, there was some violence.

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:14.810
Yes.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:16.420
And I'm not minimizing it by saying some.

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:20.300
I don't mean what I mean is in the grand scheme
of things.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:23.190
Those were advances that happened because
of peaceful protests, because of who was on

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:26.260
the Supreme Court at the time and was able
to uphold some of those changes.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:29.230
So the theoretical question of the long term
progressive movement's success, if Biden loses

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:30.720
versus if he wins, let's do it.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:32.780
When it comes to November, I can't take an
action that helps Donald Trump.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:32.792
OK, last question today.

